### During solution validation
As with any project, you’ll want to ask yourself how confident you are in your solution and if you aren’t very confident, validate your solution with users before committing to development.

You will want to select a validation method that works best for your research questions.


If your research question is about…

the usability of the UI/user flow, / prototype / consider doing a moderated or unmoderated usability test, with static or dummy LLM prompts/outputs.
The usefulness or appropriateness of the LLM output, / production feature / consider 
doing a UX bash. You’ll get answers from a larger number of participants with feedback based on actually LLM responses to their questions
Whether users will be able to craft good prompts / prototype / consider 
Asking users to write example prompts for various scenarios the feature is supposed to work for. Run those prompts through the LLM and ask them if their




UI or Conversation Flow 
If you want to see how users interact with the feature, either for usability or prompt evaluation:
   - Observe how users refine and iterate their prompts
   - Test the full conversation flow with the AI
   - Assess how users recover from errors
   - Prepare multiple expected AI responses for each prompt




 want to improve a prompt, you may need to better understand the variations that users attempt a task:
   - Have users write their own prompts for specific tasks
   - Compare different phrasings for the same intent
   - Identify common patterns in user language
   - Document unexpected prompt variations

Output Evaluation
If you want to understand what a good answer is 
   - Show users multiple AI outputs for the same prompt
   - Gather feedback on output quality and relevance
   - Assess user trust in different types of responses
   - Test error messages and recovery flows



